.TH WRITE 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
write \- write bytes to a file descriptor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.BI "ssize_t write(int " descriptor ", const void* " buffer ", size_t " byte_count ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR write ()
requests that up to
.I byte_count
bytes be written from
.I buffer
to
.IR descriptor .
The caller must provide enough readable storage for the count.
The buffer is not required to be NUL\-terminated.
.PP
Short writes are possible; the caller must handle any unwritten bytes.
This wrapper does not retry automatically.
.B STDOUT_FILENO
and
.B STDERR_FILENO
in
.B <unistd.h>
name the standard output and standard error descriptors.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, the number of bytes written is returned.
On failure, \-1 is returned and
.B errno
is set to the positive Linux error number.
Successful calls leave
.B errno
unchanged.
.SH ERRORS
Include
.B <errno.h>
to inspect
.BR errno .
Possible errors include
.BR EBADF ,
.BR EFAULT ,
.BR EINTR ,
.BR ENOSPC ,
and
.BR EPIPE .
A broken pipe can also generate
.BR SIGPIPE ;
see
.BR write (2)
for Linux behavior and other possible errors.
.SH NOTES
This Crunch function uses the Linux x86\-64 syscall and is linked from
.BR libcrunch.a .
Its
.B errno
is process\-wide, not thread\-local.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR open (3crunch),
.BR read (3crunch),
.BR close (3crunch),
.BR write (2)
